<h2>Why biodata evidence is everywhere</h2>
<p>Most arranged matches today begin on portals or through circulated biodatas, and those documents are drafted to impress: incomes rounded generously upward, family businesses described at their most prosperous, properties listed without encumbrances. When the marriage fails and maintenance is contested, the same documents resurface — now wielded by the claimant as an admission of means, and disowned by their maker as marketing. Both instincts contain a truth, and the law\'s task is to hold them in proportion.</p>

<h2>The evidentiary character of a profile figure</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>It is an admission…</h4><p>A statement of income made by a party, in a document he created or used, can be put to him and calls for an explanation. Silence or evasion lets the court draw inferences.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>…but not a measurement</h4><p>Marriage-market statements are made without verification and with an incentive to inflate. Standing alone, they are too fragile to found a finding of actual income.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>Proof must be formal</h4><p>Portal profiles are electronic records: printouts need certification under Section 63 BSA (the successor to Section 65B), and authorship or use must be established, usually in cross-examination.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Where the figure actually lands in the analysis</h2>
<p>The operative framework remains the disclosure regime consolidated in <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>: both parties file affidavits of assets, income and liabilities; quantum follows the status of the parties, reasonable needs, incomes and earning capacities, and the standard of living in the matrimonial home. Within that framework, a biodata figure does three jobs:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>It tests credibility.</strong> A man who claimed ₹3 lakh a month to prospective brides and ₹30,000 to the court must explain one of the two numbers — and the explanation colours everything else he says.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>It corroborates lifestyle evidence.</strong> Combined with proved expenditure — vehicles, travel, rentals, club memberships — the profile supports an assessment of notional income despite modest returns.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>It frames interrogatories.</strong> The claims in the profile tell the claimant\'s counsel exactly what to ask for: the business\'s bank statements, the property documents, the GST filings behind the advertised prosperity.</div>
</div>

<h2>Handling the issue from each side</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>If you are the claimant</th><th>If you are the respondent</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Preserve the profile early — dated screenshots or downloads with the certification needed for electronic records; portals delete profiles after marriage.</td><td>Do not simply deny the document; explain the figure\'s basis or its author, and reconcile it with your disclosures.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Plead it as one strand among several: returns, bank summons, lifestyle, business turnover.</td><td>File a complete, conservative and accurate affidavit — an understated affidavit beside an inflated profile is the worst combination.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Confront the maker in cross-examination and pin authorship or use.</td><td>Remember that exaggeration in a biodata is not an offence, but a false affidavit in court is — perjury exposure attaches to the sworn document.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The larger lesson</h2>
<p>Courts fixing maintenance are engaged in a reconstruction of economic reality between two people with every incentive to distort it in opposite directions. The matrimonial profile is a small, vivid piece of that reconstruction — a party\'s own portrait of his prosperity, painted when impressing rather than resisting. It will rarely decide the case alone, but it reliably decides something subtler: whom the court believes. Parties, and the families who draft biodatas for them, should write nothing in the marriage market that they cannot stand behind in a courtroom.</p>
<div class="note"><p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice. The weight given to any document depends on proof, attribution and the full evidentiary record of the case.</p></div>
